# Messaging

Every send is one call: `manage_messages.send` delivers a new message, a
reply, or a correction. The `messaging_write` executor description names that
reply capability; the exact `manage_messages.send` operation contract carries
the creation-time `reply_to` shape and mention span units; this guide carries
the cross-operation model and worked examples. Load the contract with
`describe_operation` before constructing a send.

## Origin, home, audience: three different places

A message stands in three places at once. Confusing them is the usual failure.

- **Communication origin** (`origin`) is the immutable context the message is
  authored in: either a Collection venue
  (`{"type":"collection","collection_id":"<collection-id>"}`) or an exact
  direct participant set
  (`{"type":"direct","participant_ids":["<person-a>","<person-b>"]}`). It
  decides which conversation the message belongs to. A reply or correction
  must repeat its target's origin exactly.
- **Filing home** (`home_id`) is where the message is filed and browsed. It is
  never context: filing a direct message under a Collection does not put it in
  that Collection's conversation, and a Collection-origin send must file in
  that same Collection.
- **Addressed audience** (`addressed_to`) is who carries the obligation: the
  recipient Person ids. It is neither venue nor filing.

## Addressed audience and expectation

`expectation` names the obligation a send places: `none`, `ack`, `reply`,
`action`, or `decision`. Addressing carries expectation, origin carries venue,
so an empty `addressed_to` is valid only with expectation `'none'` — an
unaddressed channel post puts an obligation on nobody and inherits the
Collection's audience. A non-`none` expectation with nobody addressed is
rejected: nobody carries the obligation.

## New messages, replies, corrections

- **New message.** Supply `body`, `origin`, `addressed_to`, `expectation`,
  `idempotency_key`, and `reason`. `idempotency_key` is unique per distinct
  send intent; reuse with a different intent is rejected.
- **Reply.** A new message plus one creation-time link:
  `links:[{"target_id":"<message-id>","relationship":"reply_to"}]`. The reply
  must retain the target message's communication origin, and at most one
  `reply_to` target is allowed. There is no dedicated reply action and no
  `reply_to_id` field: the generic creation-time link is the whole primitive.
- **Correction.** Same shape with `"relationship":"supersedes"`: the source
  replaces the target while preserving an explicit historical trail. Same
  single-target and same-origin rules as a reply.

## Immutable contextual links

`reply_to` and `supersedes` links are immutable Message content, authored at
creation. They cannot be added after creation — not by a second send, not by
`manage_links`, not by editing the record. A message sent flat stays
unthreaded; the only repair is a new, correctly threaded message. Post-send
threading repair is rejected because the reply context is part of what the
message *is*: silently re-parenting it would rewrite conversation history
under every reader who already saw the flat message.

## Deleting your own message

`manage_messages` `action:"delete"` with `message_id`, `idempotency_key` and
`reason` withdraws one Message you authored. Only the author can delete: you
need Edit on the Message, it must be owned by the Person bound to your
account, and your credential must be the actor on its creation event, so another
member who can edit the shared Collection cannot delete it,
and a Message sent as a persona can be deleted only by its owner. A refusal is
a plain error, never a silent no-op.

Delete is the record tombstone, not erasure: the Message leaves every listing,
reactions on it are refused, and its notification candidates are withdrawn, but
its body stays in stored history. The same `idempotency_key` replays the first
receipt; deleting an already-deleted Message under a new key is refused as
already deleted (to its author; anyone else sees it as missing).

A live reply may still point at a deleted parent. `list_context` keeps the
reply and returns the parent in `deleted_parents` as a body-less stub
(`id`, `deleted:true`, `body:null`, `created_at`, `deleted_at`) so a client can
render "This message was deleted" in place. A deleted Message with no live
replies appears nowhere. Stubs carry no name, author or audience, and appear
only for a parent you could see before it was deleted; seeing the reply alone
does not earn one.

## Editing your own message

`manage_messages` `action:"edit"` with `message_id`, `body`, `idempotency_key`
and `reason` (and optionally `mentions`) replaces the text of one Message you
authored. The authorship rule is exactly delete's: Edit on the Message and
ownership by the Person bound to your account, backed by the credential that
sent it (the actor recorded on the Message's creation event), so re-pointing an
account at a Person does not hand over that Person's Messages. Another member
who can edit
the shared Collection cannot rewrite it, and a Message sent as a persona can be
edited only by its owner. Refusals are plain errors.

An edit is recorded as a `message.revision.recorded.v1` event on the same
Message record; the generic `update_record` path still refuses to change a
Message. The record's body becomes the new text and every earlier text stays in
the history (`record.created` and each earlier revision). A client shows
"(edited)" by counting those events in the `content_events` relation:
`SELECT count(*), max(created_at_ms) FROM content_events WHERE record_id=? AND
type='message.revision.recorded.v1'`. The receipt carries `edited_at` and
`edit_count`.

Nothing re-alerts: read state, awareness and notification candidates are
untouched, and candidates carry no text so none can go stale. Structured
mention spans index the body, so an edit re-establishes them. With `mentions`
(same shape as send) the given set replaces the old one, but it may only name
people the Message already mentioned: a new target would be a new alert, so it
is refused and needs a new Message. Without `mentions`, existing mentions are relocated into the new text, so a
typo fix ahead of an @mention does not lose it. In the order of their current
spans, each mention's authored label is searched for after the previous match
(from that match's start if the original spans overlapped, so overlapping
mentions of different people both survive; otherwise from its end), and its span
becomes the match's UTF-8 byte range. If a label appears more than once,
matching is in order: the first acceptable occurrence after the previous match
wins. An occurrence is acceptable only if it is not inside a fenced or inline
code span or a URL (`http://` or `https://` up to whitespace), and it is bounded
as the author wrote it: where the original mention stood against a non-word
character or the edge of the body, so must the new one (Unicode letters, digits
and `_` are word characters), so `Bob` never relocates into `Bobby`; and a label
that followed an `@` must follow one again. Anything not found drops the
mention. Targets never change and nobody is added. When an edit retires a
mention, every outstanding mention candidate proposed for a dropped person is
withdrawn (the person is the one the candidate's account was bound to when it
was proposed, read from the audited binding ledger in act order, with the
pre-cutover rows first).

Refused: a deleted Message (named to its author, missing to anyone else), a
blank body, a body identical to the current one, a Message whose audience was
never declared, and a federated copy. The same `idempotency_key` replays the
first receipt; the key on a different body is a conflict. Body size is bounded
exactly as for send.

## Principal and record mentions

`mentions` point at immutable prose from inside the same send.

- `target_kind` is `principal` (a person) or `record` (any record).
- A principal mention target must already be addressed in `addressed_to`,
  with one exception: in a Collection-origin send you may mention a person
  who can see that Collection without addressing them. The mention alerts
  them; it adds no obligation, makes them no addressee, and does not narrow
  who can read the post. A person who cannot see the Collection is refused.
  Direct (DM) sends keep the addressed rule: a direct context's readers are
  its exact participant set.
- Each span is zero-based and half-open over UTF-8 bytes:
  `[span_start, span_end)` into `body`. Byte offsets, not character counts:
  both ends must be UTF-8 character boundaries and
  `body[span_start..span_end]` must equal `authored_label` byte-for-byte,
  including multibyte text. `span_end` must be greater than `span_start`, and
  `mention_id` values must be non-empty and unique within the send.

## Reading messages: inbox, direct contexts, conversations

`manage_messages.list_inbox` is the viewer-relative read for "my messages".
`view: "browse"` lists every Message the viewer can see, **including the
viewer's own sent Messages** (their `attention.unread` is false); `needs_me`
and `all_new` leave sent Messages out because they carry no obligation and are
never unread. Pages come from one pinned `snapshot`; continue with
`after: next_after`. Today the snapshot covers every visible Message (at most
10,000, otherwise the call refuses), so prefer `view: "needs_me"` or a
`conversation_id` when you only need a slice.

Since `native.message-inbox.v3` each item also carries `sender`,
`participants`, `participant_count`, `reply_to_id` and `conversation`. They
are resolved for the returned page when you fetch it, under your access at
that moment, not frozen into the snapshot: a person or reply target you lose
sight of between pages disappears from the later pages.

- `sender` and each `participants[]` entry are `{person_id, name, viewer,
  state:"person"}`. Use `viewer: true` to find your own slot; your own slot
  has `name: null` if you cannot read your own Person record. A sender you
  cannot see is the opaque `{person_id: null, name: null, viewer: false,
  state: "hidden"}`. Account ids are never shown.
- `participants` lists a direct context's participants **you can see**,
  yourself first, then by Person id, at most 8. A member you cannot see has
  no slot and is not counted: `participant_count` is the number you can see
  (before the 8 cap), so group size is not disclosed beyond that. A legacy
  Message (no declared origin) has no stored members, so it lists its sender
  and `addressed_to` audience under the same rules. A note to self lists just
  you. It is empty for a Collection Message.
- `reply_to_id` is the Message this replies to, or `null` when there is none
  or you cannot see it.
- `conversation` is `{type, key?}`. A `collection` Message has
  `key: "collection:<id>"` only when you can see that Collection, otherwise
  just `{type: "collection"}`. A `direct` Message has **no key** yet: group
  direct Messages by the participants you can see for now. A stable direct
  conversation id (opaque and persisted) is planned as its own change. A
  `legacy` Message has `{type: "legacy"}`. Your own Message authored in Unfiled (origin collection `native:unfiled`)
  with no addressee is `{type: "self"}` (a note to self).

Hosted and Desktop (kernel v2) answer with the same schema and the same
identity rules. Desktop items no longer carry the raw `origin` or
`communication_origin` fields, which listed every participant.

**`scope: "direct"`** limits `list_inbox` to your own direct contexts: Messages
whose declared direct origin names you (under any principal you have held,
including retained aliases), plus legacy Messages (no declared origin) in
Unfiled that you sent or were addressed in, plus your **notes to self**: your
own Messages (owned by your Person, never merely sent by a principal that
now resolves to you) authored in Unfiled (origin collection `native:unfiled`)
with no `addressed_to`, whose Collection origin would otherwise exclude them (`conversation: {type: "self"}`, `participants` = you,
`participant_count: 1`). Someone else's unaddressed Messages are never
included, and neither are channel posts.
Hosted selects them before any visibility or hydration work. Notes to self
come from a keyed read of your own Messages in Unfiled, so other people's
notes and your own channel posts do not scale it. Legacy Messages are found
from your own sender and `addressed_to` audience rows, so that part costs
O(your own sent and addressed Messages), not a constant: it grows with your
traffic, never with other people's activity. On Desktop (kernel
v2) selection is one scan of the stored message states (no kernel index yet),
so it is O(messages in the workspace) with a small cost per row; the
expensive per-message work still runs only for your DMs.

Visibility is applied before the cap. It keeps at most 2000 **readable**
candidates, newest first; unreadable or deleted Messages never use up the cap.
The response carries `scope`, `candidate_cap` and `truncated`; `truncated:
true` means more readable direct Messages exist than were kept. Which
Messages a view contains is decided when the snapshot is built; every page,
on every view, is hydrated when you fetch it, so a Message edited between
pages shows its current content. A cursor is bound to its scope. `scope`
cannot be combined with `conversation_id`, and a caller with no current member
identity gets an error, not an empty list.
`preview_chars` (1 to 1000) cuts each `body` and sets `body_truncated`; it
defaults to 360 with `scope: "direct"` and is off (full body) otherwise.

`manage_memberships.list` with `include_person: true` adds `person_name` to
each member and shows `person_id` only for People you can see; a member whose
Person you cannot see has `person_id: null` and `person_name: null`. It is
served on hosted databases, not on a Desktop local node (which has no
membership catalogue).

`manage_messages.list_my_conversations` is **not** a direct-message list. It
counts only Collection conversations that classified, readable Messages
belong to, scanning all Messages with no paging, so a direct context never
appears in it. Use `list_inbox` and group by `conversation.key` for direct
contexts.

## Private read preferences

Use `manage_messages.set_preference` with `preference: "mark_read"` or
`"mark_unread"`, one visible `message_id`, `expected_version`,
`idempotency_key`, and `reason`. Read the current preference version from
`list_message_state.messages[].attention.version` (zero before any preference
write). A successful receipt returns `version = expected_version + 1` and
`unread_override` false or true. An exact retry returns that original receipt
before checking a stale version; it does not undo a later write.

`attention.unread` and `my_message_state.unread` are the authoritative unread
value. Human `stage` records monotonic awareness; marking unread changes neither
opened/acknowledged history nor obligations. A missing or null override uses
legacy awareness-derived unread. Archive and own human-authored Messages remain
read, while persona-spoken Messages use the ordinary unread rule. Other
preference actions preserve the override. These are caller-private writes and
reads: they do not change shared message content or another viewer's state.

## Arrival notifications

A direct-context Message notifies each other participant, and a reply notifies
the thread's participants (the root's author plus everyone who has replied),
with a `routine_arrival` notification candidate. The sender is never notified,
and a recipient who already holds a mention or obligation candidate for the
Message keeps just that one. Muting any Message of a thread, or of the exact
direct participant set, silences later arrivals in that conversation.

`manage_messages.get_notification_preference` reads the caller's own arrival
preference; `manage_messages.set_notification_preference` sets `arrival`, with
`idempotency_key` and `reason`:

- `"all"` (default): DMs and thread replies notify.
- `"dms_and_mentions"`: only Messages sent in a direct context (DMs) notify,
  and that includes a reply inside a DM. Replies in a channel thread do not.
- `"off"`: no DM or thread-reply arrivals.

The preference governs these arrivals only. Mentions, Messages that put an
expectation on you, reminders you set and policy interventions always notify,
whatever the preference. It is checked when the candidate is created and again
when delivery harvests it, so a change also withholds arrivals already waiting.

## Complete examples

A direct-message reply with a mention after multibyte prose. `é` is two UTF-8
bytes, so in `"Héllo recipient"` the word `recipient` spans bytes 7..16:

```json
{
  "action": "send",
  "body": "Héllo recipient",
  "origin": {"type": "direct", "participant_ids": ["<person-a>", "<person-b>"]},
  "addressed_to": ["<person-b>"],
  "expectation": "reply",
  "links": [{"target_id": "<root-message-id>", "relationship": "reply_to"}],
  "mentions": [{"mention_id": "mention-1", "target_kind": "principal", "target_id": "<person-b>", "span_start": 7, "span_end": 16, "authored_label": "recipient"}],
  "idempotency_key": "dm-reply-1",
  "reason": "Reply to <person-b> in our direct context"
}
```

A Collection channel post and a reply to it. `✓` is three UTF-8 bytes, so in
`"✓ recipient confirmed"` the word `recipient` spans bytes 4..13:

```json
{
  "action": "send",
  "body": "Weekly notes are up",
  "origin": {"type": "collection", "collection_id": "<collection-id>"},
  "addressed_to": [],
  "expectation": "none",
  "home_id": "<collection-id>",
  "idempotency_key": "channel-root-1",
  "reason": "Post the weekly notes to the project channel"
}
```

```json
{
  "action": "send",
  "body": "✓ recipient confirmed",
  "origin": {"type": "collection", "collection_id": "<collection-id>"},
  "addressed_to": ["<person-b>"],
  "expectation": "ack",
  "home_id": "<collection-id>",
  "links": [{"target_id": "<channel-post-id>", "relationship": "reply_to"}],
  "mentions": [{"mention_id": "mention-1", "target_kind": "principal", "target_id": "<person-b>", "span_start": 4, "span_end": 13, "authored_label": "recipient"}],
  "idempotency_key": "channel-reply-1",
  "reason": "Confirm the notes to <person-b> in the project channel"
}
```

## Failure modes

- **Cross-context reply.** A `reply_to` target from another origin is refused:
  replies must retain the communication origin in which they are authored.
  Re-address the send into the target's origin.
- **Two `reply_to` targets.** At most one canonical `reply_to` (and at most
  one `supersedes`) target per message. Split the intent across messages.
- **Mention span mismatch.** Offsets past the body end, ends that split a
  multibyte character, or a slice that differs from `authored_label` are
  refused. Count bytes, not characters, and copy the slice exactly.
- **Unaddressed principal mention.** In a direct send, a principal mention
  whose target is not in `addressed_to` is refused: address them or drop the
  mention. In a Collection-origin send it is allowed only for a person who can
  see that Collection; otherwise it is refused, and granting them access is a
  separate, deliberate step.
- **Flat send sent by mistake.** The message cannot be re-parented. Send a new
  reply with a creation-time `reply_to` link; the flat message remains as
  authored history.


## Persona identity and message speakers

`manage_participants.register_persona` creates an owner-private `Entity/persona`
profile (name, body and open facets), or registers an existing owned profile by
`record_id` only if it is already owner-only. When using `record_id`, omit
name, body and home_id; edit those through ordinary record operations first.
`manage_participants.list` lists the caller's active and retired
personas. `manage_participants.retire_persona` preserves identity and history.
A registered persona cannot change owner, type, placement or policy, or be
deleted. Edit its name, body and open facets with ordinary record operations.
Personas never hold credentials or receive policy grants.

`manage_messages.send` accepts advisory `speaker: {persona_id}`. A live owned
persona produces an immutable speaker fact with engine-selected
`client_asserted` assurance; its Message author remains the human owner.
After ordinary permission checks, invalid, foreign, nonexistent, retired or
malformed claims are refused with one common `speaker not found` error. A claim
never grants authority or changes permission evaluation. Self-addressing is
allowed only with a valid speaker. Every send receipt, including an idempotent
retry, reports `speaker_claim_status: recorded|absent` for the committed Message.
Retries validate any supplied claim and preserve the original speaker fact;
retirement refuses new claims, including claims supplied on retries.
A persona may speak in
any thread its owner can write, under that thread's normal visibility. Its
current display name on a visible utterance does not share the private persona
profile. Renaming a persona changes its displayed name on historical Messages;
the stored speaker identity and assurance remain immutable.
Non-message writes have no speaker. No agent address or participant DM/inbox
operation is provided by these slices; run keys remain correlation only.

Caller-visible SQL `my_participants` lists the caller's personas;
`message_speakers` exposes identity and assurance on visible Messages.
`my_message_state.is_own` is true only for a human-spoken Message owned by the
caller; a persona-spoken Message uses the ordinary caller-private unread
rule. `get_attention` uses the same authorship distinction. Persona-spoken
replies never count as human recipient evidence. A human reply with an explicit `reply_to` in the same
Collection can satisfy that owner's obligation to a persona utterance.
Speaker-bearing Messages, including self-addressed ones, are explicitly refused
by federation export (`participant_message_not_federatable`); the v1 peer wire
has no speaker field and continues to refuse ordinary sender-in-audience input.
Canonical interchange preserves these Messages, audiences and speaker facts.
